  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 239 AM CST Wed Nov 4 2026\n\nToday - Thursday: On and Off Showers Continue\n\nContinued rainfall is likely through Thursday as an upper-level \nclosed low begins to manifest across the Upper Midwest as \ndeterministic guidance suggests that this feature will slowly \nprogress eastward over the next couple of days. As a result, the \ngrand ensemble (GEFS/EC/Canadian ensemble) has very high \nprobabilities (50-90%) for measurable rainfall during any 6-hr \nthrough Friday morning. Overall given the fairly weak forcing with \nthe surface low overhead and the southerly moisture plume to our \neast, thinking that additional precipitation amounts should be on \nthe lighter side with the grand ensemble (GEFS/EC/Canadian ensemble) \nonly having medium probabilities (20-50%) for rainfall amounts over \n0.5\" for today through Thursday. In any case, with the frontal \npassage associated with the low pressure center moving through the \nregion, temperatures will be noticeably cooler through Thursday with \nhighs in the 40s and 50s. \n\nFriday - Sunday: Drier and Cooler\n\nBy the time Friday and the weekend rolls around, the upper-level low \nresponsible for much of our showery pattern this week pushes into \nthe eastern Great Lakes and allows for shortwave upper-level ridging \nto move into the Upper Midwest. Consequently, expecting skies to \nbegin to clear late Friday and into Saturday. As a result, overnight \nlows will likely fall into the 20s for much of the region by \nSaturday morning with surface high pressure building into the \nregion. However, in response to the upper-level ridging, would \nexpect temperatures to gradually warm with highs returning in to the \nlower to middle 50s. \n\nEarly Next Week: \n\nAs we head into early next week, zonal to northwesterly flow with \nweak upper-level ridging for Monday will allow for some additional \nwarming with highs in the 50s to lower 60s. However, as flow \ncontinues to turn more zonal to quasi-northwesterly, temperatures \nwill likely remain steady-state, being slightly above normal. \nAdditionally, with the absence of any profound shortwave troughing \nduring this timeframe, the NBM unsurprisingly has minimal \nprecipitation chances at this time.",
